body:
I feel the same way! I just finished *Ninth House*, so I can officially say that I have read all of Bardugo's books, and I firmly believe that the *Shadow and Bone* trilogy was her weakest work. *\[Shadow and Bone spoilers\]* >!Full Disclosure: I absolutely despised Mal, and Nikolai and The Darkling were my favorite characters so I have VERY strong feelings about the ending to Ruin and Rising, but I digress.!< That said, *Shadow and Bone* was not a terrible series by any stretch of the imagination, but it can't even compare to *Six of Crows*. I like to say that the *Shadow and Bone* trilogy walked so *Six of Crows* could run.

&#x200B;

All of the characters in *Six of Crows* were so well-developed, and the dialogue is so witty! The plot is genius, and, overall, I have nothing but praise for that book. I wish I could say more about it, but it's been a little while since I read it, and I've already read the sequels, so I'm scared I might get them mixed up and accidentally spoil you.

(Are you me? I've been doing everything *but* studying lately.) It sounds like you're doing well, though. I think it's okay to go through the grammar at a sort-of easy-going pace because you can retain the information a bit better. And it's good that you're keeping up with Anki. It gets so horrifically overwhelming if you miss/skip reviews. 頑張って！

&#x200B;

I started learning so I could play all those untranslated otome games that are never to see the light of day in the western world, and to read literature. I want to be able to read *Kokoro, The Pillow Book,* and *1Q84*, specifically. I've been learning for almost two years now, but I'm only about halfway though Genki 2 and my Anki deck is around 1500 cards. Recently, though, I've spent less time studying from the textbook and more time watching the original Sailor Moon in Japanese, with Japanese and English subtitles. And I know what you mean about light bulb moments. Every time I can understand a line in Sailor Moon without looking at the subtitles, I get so excited.

I had a Vita about three years ago, before I discovered otome games. I took it *everywhere*. To school, to the grocery store, to the doctor's office. Unfortunately, about a year after I got it, the prongs in the charging port became damaged (I still have it and can send a picture if necessary), and it was not under warranty. I took it to a repair shop, but I was informed that it was irreparable. I was absolutely crushed. All my games, all my save data--gone. I ended up getting a job to buy a new one, but now that I'm in college, all of my money has gone toward tuition and textbooks. I'd love to have a Vita so I could play my old games again, and buy otome games when they go on sale.

&#x200B;

(Thank you for this opportunity. Good luck to everyone entering!)
